Step 1: Generate a Blowfish key
Open Command Prompt or the VS Code terminal and run:
keytool -genseckey -alias blowfishkey -keyalg Blowfish -keysize 128 -keystore blowfish.jceks -storetype JCEKS


Enter and confirm a keystore password when prompted. If prompted for a key password, you can use the same password.

This creates blowfish.jceks, which stores your secret key.
Step 2: Create the Java encryption program
Create a file named BlowfishEncryption.java in VS Code.

import javax.crypto.Cipher;
import javax.crypto.SecretKey;
import java.security.KeyStore;
import java.util.Base64;

public class BlowfishEncryption {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {

        // Load the keystore
        KeyStore ks = KeyStore.getInstance("JCEKS");
        char[] password = System.console() != null
                ? System.console().readPassword("Keystore password: ")
                : new java.util.Scanner(System.in)
                    .nextLine().toCharArray();

        try (java.io.FileInputStream fis =
                     new java.io.FileInputStream("blowfish.jceks")) {
            ks.load(fis, password);
        }

        // Retrieve the Blowfish secret key
        KeyStore.ProtectionParameter protection =
                new KeyStore.PasswordProtection(password);
        KeyStore.SecretKeyEntry entry =
                (KeyStore.SecretKeyEntry) ks.getEntry(
                        "blowfishkey", protection);
        SecretKey key = entry.getSecretKey();

        // Encrypt the message
        String message = "Hello world";
        Cipher cipher = Cipher.getInstance("Blowfish");
        cipher.init(Cipher.ENCRYPT_MODE, key);

        byte[] encrypted = cipher.doFinal(
                message.getBytes(java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets.UTF_8));

        String encryptedText =
                Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(encrypted);

        System.out.println("Original text: " + message);
        System.out.println("Encrypted text: " + encryptedText);
    }
}



Step 3: Compile and run
Run these commands in the terminal from the folder containing both files:
javac BlowfishEncryption.java
java BlowfishEncryption


Enter the keystore password you created in Step 1.

Your output will look like this:
Keystore password:
Original text: Hello world
Encrypted text: <Base64-encoded ciphertext>


The encrypted value will vary because the key is randomly generated.
Step 4: Verify the key
You can check that your key is stored in the keystore with:
keytool -list -v -keystore blowfish.jceks -storetype JCEKS
